A texture on Android shown from one HardwareBuffer its producer makes and
keeps (board the-galaxy-tab-canvas-went-slow-on-build-1095, 길 A; 유저
2026-11-22: 「윈도랑 안드랑 다른거 통일못하는거야?」 — 「그게 젤
근본해결」). On every other platform Anicel's engine makes two surfaces
and Flutter shows each as a texture of its own, turn about; on Android the
only doors take an Image from a queue (SurfaceProducer, ImageTextureEntry),
which an app cannot make, so the engine copied each frame onto a queue's
next image.

TextureRegistry.createHardwareBufferTexture(buffer) makes an entry of that
one buffer, for its life: the native side takes it through the image
texture's own door (registerImageTexture), asks the entry whether it is a
HardwareBufferConsumer, and imports the buffer once, as it imports an
Image's (ImageExternalTexture::AcquireLatestHardwareBuffer). An entry that
is not one takes the Image path unchanged. Nothing waits for the buffer to
be written: the producer shows the texture once its GPU has finished it,
and writes it again only while it is not shown.

No buffer is handed under an id that already has one: a picture changes by
the layer tree's texture id, so the raster never picks up a picture a frame
apart from where the tree placed it (board
panning-then-pen-jumps-the-android-canvas-a-frame).

